"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, and lack of lymphovascular invasion. The surgical pathology showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pT2c stage with no extraprostatic extension. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 5.2 and the absence of lymph node removal, which means nodal status is unknown, widening the uncertainty. The biopsy findings showed a Gleason 3+4 and 3+3, with the prostatectomy specimen showing a Gleason 3+4. The lack of documented postoperative PSA or follow-up significantly limits the ability to assess the subsequent trajectory. Missing nodal sampling further increases this uncertainty. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory would be crucial to refine this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 74.38 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 44.63–104.13 months; its coverage has not been validated."
